  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nA Genetic Local Search (GLS) algorithm will replace the local coordinate descent approach. This method combines the global exploration capabilities of genetic algorithms with the local refinement optimization steps to find a lower C5 value. GLS is chosen because it excels at adapting to complex, multi-modal landscapes and constraints, which is common in the Erd\u0151s minimal overlap problem. The algorithm will explore different scales and then refine high-quality solutions found at the target resolution. The main limitations are the expensive objective function evaluations and the need for careful hyperparameter tuning to avoid premature convergence.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Objective: Minimize C5(h) = (2/n) * max_k [np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k]]\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211h_i = n/2\n- Representation: The solution is represented as a piecewise constant function, where we consider only aligned shifts (k being multiples of dx) with dx=2/n.\n- Hyperparameter Surrogates: We'll use a hierarchical approach with different hyperparameter regimes depending on the scale. HasDiag is not used because with n=700, the problem is already large enough that diagonal repair is not needed.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a) Parent Validation: Directly use `initial_h_values` if available. Otherwise, generate a valid parent via random projection.\n   b) Initialization: Create a population of candidate solutions by projecting random states to feasible set. Use a combination of:\n        i) Parent-based mutations with Gaussian perturbations\n        ii) Coarse-to-fine interpolation if parent is available\n        iii) Random base solutions for initialization\n   c) Core Update:\n        i) Crossover Population Deep Copy\n        ii) Repair each candidate to feasible set\n        iii) Mutation using Covariance Matrix Adaptation (CMA-ES) style adaptation\n        iv) Evaluation using max correlation calculation\n        v) Selection by fitness (lower C5 is better) with tournament selection and elitism\n   d) Hierarchical Variations:\n        i) Coarse-to-Fine (for convergence): \n           - Start at n=20 and refine to n=700\n           - Use hierarchical transfer for centroid candidates\n        ii) Predefined Cycles:\n           Cycle 1: Coarse search at n=20\n           Cycle 2: Medium search at n=400\n           Cycle 3: Fine search at n=700\n           Cycle 4: Hierarchical finalization\n        iii) Multi-start Strategy:\n           - Use either:\n             Version A: CMA-ES inspired mutation with repair\n             Version B: Simulated Annealing for coarse properties\n           Rotation between versions if stuck\n   e) Local Refinement:\n        - Apply coordinate descent iterations after each hierarchical transfer\n        - Use SLSQP optimization as fallback if coordinate descent doesn't improve\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n   a) Primary Variation 1: Hierarchical Search Schedule\n        Cycle 1 (20 points):\n          Behavior: Wide mutation range\n          Target scale factor: n=20\n          Start time: Immediate\n          Deadline: 500s\n        Cycle 2 (400 points):\n          Behavior: Mid-range mutation\n          Target scale factor: n=400\n          Start time: After Cycle 1\n          Deadline: 400s\n        Cycle 3 (700 points):\n          Behavior: Fine mutation with repair\n          Target scale factor: n=700\n          Start time: After Cycle 2\n        Transition from nme:\n          - Use linear interpolation to fill gaps\n          - Project to maintain sum and bounds\n\n   b) Secondary Variation 1: Optimization Schedule Rotation\n        Version A (default): CMA-style mutation\n        Version B: Simulated Annealing with focus on smoothness\n        Timer-based rotation: After 5 cycles switch to other version\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   a) Hierarchical Schedule:\n        Sleep per cycle: 50s\n        Evaluation limit per cycle: 500*population size\n        Deadline checks: Post-evaluation per candidate\n        Start limit: If start time exceeded, fall back to SLSQP\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   a) Feasibility Check:\n        * Verify sum(h) \u2248 350\n        * Verify all 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n   b) Objective Calculation:\n        Re-calculate C5 to ensure consistency\n        Deal with NaNs by reverting\n   c) Final Selection:\n        * Best candidate at n=700 after either GA or SLSQP\n        * Re-run local optimization if improves\n        * Return as tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
